From: omacut-windows prepare-source <prepare-source@omacut-windows.invalid>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 14:57:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Run upstream's tests on Qt 6.8

Upstream tests against Arch's Qt, which is newer than the 6.8 the port
builds with. Two of its tests rely on what only the newer Qt does:

- dropUrls() builds a QDragEnterEvent from a QPointF, a constructor Qt
  6.8 does not have. The point is a whole pixel anyway, so pass it as a
  QPoint.
- qmlTimelineViewDependsOnlyOnTheEdit compares a grab of the edited bar
  with a fresh editor's. Qt 6.8's software renderer, which the tests run
  on offscreen, can keep a node from before an edit on screen until the
  bar's geometry changes, so the grab showed a clip frame that the scene
  no longer held. Hiding the bar, grabbing once, and showing it again
  rebuilds its nodes. The grab matters: it renders a frame while the bar
  is hidden, which a fixed wait did not always do on a slower machine
  (the macOS runner). What the test compares is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 tests/omacut_tests.cpp | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/omacut_tests.cpp b/tests/omacut_tests.cpp
index 41512de2daaa46f3f3ae14aa953d05930e1bf661..2c310931da147a05b67b75a3dceabbd1e294fcfe 100644
--- a/tests/omacut_tests.cpp
+++ b/tests/omacut_tests.cpp
@@ -3600,7 +3600,8 @@ static bool dropUrls(QQuickWindow *window, const QList<QUrl> &urls) {
     QMimeData mime;
     mime.setUrls(urls);
     const QPointF point(window->width() / 2, window->height() / 3);
-    QDragEnterEvent enter(point, Qt::CopyAction, &mime, Qt::LeftButton, Qt::NoModifier);
+    // Qt 6.8 has only a QPoint constructor for this one; the point is whole anyway.
+    QDragEnterEvent enter(point.toPoint(), Qt::CopyAction, &mime, Qt::LeftButton, Qt::NoModifier);
     QCoreApplication::sendEvent(window, &enter);
     if (!enter.isAccepted()) {
         QDragLeaveEvent leave;
@@ -3923,6 +3924,15 @@ public:
             v.frames << videoOf.value(prefix, prefix) + frame.mid(prefix.size());
         }
         v.frames.sort();
+        // Qt 6.8's software renderer, which these tests run on, can keep a
+        // node from before an edit on screen until the bar's geometry changes:
+        // a clip's frame where no clip is any more. Rebuilding the bar's nodes
+        // first makes the picture show what the scene holds. That takes a
+        // frame rendered while the bar is hidden, and a grab renders one then
+        // and there; a fixed wait was not always long enough on a slow machine.
+        bar->setVisible(false);
+        (void)window->grabWindow();
+        bar->setVisible(true);
         const QImage whole = window->grabWindow();
         const qreal scale = whole.devicePixelRatio();
         const QRectF rect = bar->mapRectToScene(QRectF(0, 0, bar->width(), bar->height()));
